{"name":"Art Nouveau Glass","hex":"#a29aa0","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"art","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-5 language code for artificial languages."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The conscious production or arrangement of sounds, colours, forms, movements, or other elements in a manner that affects the senses and emotions, usually specifically the production of the beautiful in a graphic or plastic medium.","The creative and emotional expression of mental imagery, such as visual, auditory, social, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/art"},{"term":"nouveau","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["New, fashionable."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A wine released in the same year that the grapes were harvested to make it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nouveau"},{"term":"glass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An amorphous solid, often transparent substance, usually made by melting silica sand with various additives (for most purposes, a mixture of soda, potash and lime is added).","Any amorphous solid (one without a regular crystal lattice)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fit with glass; to glaze.","To enclose in glass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Art Nouveau glass","blocks":[{"text":"Art Nouveau glass is fine glass art in the Art Nouveau style. Typically the forms are undulating, sinuous and colorful art, usually inspired by natural forms. Pieces are generally larger than drinking glasses, and decorative rather than practical, other than for use as vases and lighting fittings; there is little tableware. Prominent makers, from the 1890s onwards, are in France René Lalique, Emile Gallé and the Daum brothers, the American Louis Comfort Tiffany, Christopher Dresser in Scotland and England, and Friedrich Zitzman, Karl Koepping and Max Ritter von Spaun in Germany. Art Nouveau glass included decorative objects, vases, lamps, and stained glass windows. It was usually made by hand, and was usually colored with metal oxides while in a molten state in a furnace."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Coupe_B%C3%A9gonia_rose.jpg/330px-Coupe_B%C3%A9gonia_rose.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Nouveau_glass"},{"title":"Art Nouveau","blocks":[{"text":"Art Nouveau is an international style of art, architecture, and applied art, especially the decorative arts. It was often inspired by natural forms such as the sinuous curves of plants and flowers. Other characteristics of Art Nouveau were a sense of dynamism and movement, often given by asymmetry or whiplash lines, and the use of modern materials, particularly iron, glass, ceramics and later concrete, to create unusual forms and larger open spaces. It was popular between 1890 and 1910 during the Belle Époque period, and was a reaction against the academicism, eclecticism and historicism of 19th-century architecture and decorative art."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Abbesses.JPG/330px-Abbesses.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Nouveau"},{"title":"Art","blocks":[{"text":"Art is a diverse range of cultural activity centered around works utilizing creative or imaginative talents, which are expected to evoke a worthwhile experience, generally through an expression of emotional power, conceptual ideas, technical proficiency, or beauty."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Art-portrait-collage_2.jpg/330px-Art-portrait-collage_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art"},{"title":"Nouveau","blocks":[{"text":"A nouveau, or vin (de) primeur, is a wine which may be sold in the same year in which it was harvested."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nouveau"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Favrile.jpg/500px-Favrile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Favrile.jpg/500px-Favrile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFavrile.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Johann_loetz_witwe%2C_vasi_iridescenti%2C_1900.jpg/500px-Johann_loetz_witwe%2C_vasi_iridescenti%2C_1900.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Johann_loetz_witwe%2C_vasi_iridescenti%2C_1900.jpg/1280px-Johann_loetz_witwe%2C_vasi_iridescenti%2C_1900.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Johann_loetz_witwe%2C_vasi_iridescenti%2C_1900.jpg/500px-Johann_loetz_witwe%2C_vasi_iridescenti%2C_1900.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohann_loetz_witwe%2C_vasi_iridescenti%2C_1900.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Petit_vase_craquel%C3%A9.jpg/250px-Petit_vase_craquel%C3%A9.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Petit_vase_craquel%C3%A9.jpg/500px-Petit_vase_craquel%C3%A9.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Petit_vase_craquel%C3%A9.jpg/250px-Petit_vase_craquel%C3%A9.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APetit_vase_